    priorityVideoIds: ['dmUhnYfcaZd06j7eZs8q', 'K1x3PgdWg21Bpn7ZqHrV'],
    queryKeywords: ['combat', 'martial arts', 'punch', 'kick', 'fight', 'action', 'sword', 'acrobatics'],
    sections: [
      { heading: 'Balance & Center of Gravity in Combat', body: 'Watch how fighters reposition their rear foot to anchor hip torque before delivering a punch or roundhouse kick. A strike without grounded footwork feels weak and weightless.' },
      { heading: 'Impact Reaction & Camera Staging', body: 'Impact is sold by the defender’s recoil, micro-freezes, and sudden shifts in silhouette. Frame key impact hits along dynamic diagonal lines for maximum kinetic punch.' }, 
      {"heading":"Impact Deceleration & Reversal Dynamics at 24 FPS","body":"High-impact physical strikes follow an asymmetric timing profile: anticipation accelerates over 3-6 frames, the forward strike travels across 2 frames (often utilizing single-frame smears or perspective distortion), and the recovery settle extends across 8-14 frames. The apex of impact must decelerate sharply; holding the contact pose for more than 1 frame produces a wooden, unconvincing strike, while bouncing off the target immediately conveys elastic kinetic energy."}, 
      {"heading":"Weapon Momentum, Leverage & Follow-Through","body":"Weapons must never behave as weightless extensions of the hand bone. Track the weapon tip's inertia: during chambering and initial acceleration, the tip lags behind the wrist. At the moment of maximum release, the blade or staff whips past the hands, pulling the character's shoulders and hips into follow-through overshoot. Always preserve clear edge alignment along the cutting arc."}, 
      {"heading":"Common Animator Pitfalls to Avoid","body":"Avoid linear translation curves on the root bone in 3D rigs; human hips decelerate as they rise toward the up pose and accelerate into the down pose. In 2D hand-drawn animation, ensure that the foot roll stays planted without phantom slipping on the contact frames. Always trace the heel and toe arcs: the foot should lift with a smooth parabolic trajectory rather than snapping abruptly into the passing phase."}],
